  "findings": [
    "Neumaier-state correctly rounded 48/48 measured runs in both runtimes; scalar Neumaier 42/48; naive scalar 0/48.",
    "Free-threaded mode had 36/48 measured runs with process-CPU/wall ratio above 1.05; GIL-enabled mode had 0/48. Native thread identity matched configured worker count in all 16 cases per runtime.",
    "Free-threaded Neumaier-state median speedup was 1.2589x versus the matching serial Neumaier baseline; worker-count medians were 0.7733x, 1.2125x, 1.3546x and 1.3090x for 1/2/4/8 workers.",
    "GIL-enabled Neumaier-state median speedup was 0.7576x versus the matching serial Neumaier baseline; median process-CPU/wall ratio was approximately 1.003.",
    "Scalar Neumaier partial merge lost correct rounding in 6/48 cases, while merging the full (sum, correction) state retained 48/48.",
    "Serial math.fsum was substantially faster than Python-threaded Neumaier-state on these fixtures; no overall algorithm speed advantage is claimed."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "One hosted run per runtime, three repetitions per configuration and two deterministic synthetic cancellation layouts only.",
    "The GIL-enabled and free-threaded jobs ran on different hosted VM platform families (AWS versus Azure), so direct cross-runtime timing comparison is confounded.",
    "Speedup is relative to matching serial Neumaier, not the fastest serial method; serial math.fsum was substantially faster.",
    "tracemalloc reports Python-traced allocations only, not RSS, native allocations or hardware DRAM bandwidth.",
    "No independent physical x86-64 or ARM64 replication, representative application workload, confidence interval or independent numerical-method review has been completed."
  ]
